WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.071 --> 00:00:05.828
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.828 --> 00:00:10.188
A couple months ago I received a text
message from an unknown number.

00:00:10.188 --> 00:00:14.370
Now usually I don't respond to unknown
numbers but this was different.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:17.502
The text read, hey Miss Edwards,

00:00:17.502 --> 00:00:22.580
it's Sade I immediately respond back,
where are you?

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:24.290
How are you?

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:26.570
Are you still in school?

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.010
And then I flash back to
a moment about two years ago when

00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:36.200
I was working as director of operations
of a charter school in North New Jersey.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:41.160
Sade was one of my students, and
we formed a very special bond.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:45.100
But on this particular day Sade
had been kicked out of school for

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:46.440
cursing out the school principal.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.840
Now I had a feeling there
was more to the story.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.430
So I took it upon myself to leave
the school building and go find her, and

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:54.800
find out what really happened.

00:00:56.130 --> 00:01:00.545
So I walk out the building and
as soon as I step outside I see

00:01:00.545 --> 00:01:06.100
Sade sitting on the steps of the church
next to the school and she's crying.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:13.620
So I walk over to Sade and I sit down
next to her, ask her what's going on.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.790
I soon find out that
Sade wasn't particularly

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:20.620
angry with the school principal that day.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.830
She just really, really missed her dad.

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:30.180
You see the year before Sade's
father had passed away.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:33.590
But her family was too poor
to afford a tombstone, so

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:38.735
her father was cremated,
against both his and his family's wishes.

00:01:38.735 --> 00:01:45.780
Sade/e was upset that day because on that
day she just wanted a physical place

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:51.790
to remember her father and
she had nothing,so she was upset.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:57.220
So at this point I look at Sade and
I tell her Sade give me your phone.

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:00.730
I'm going to call your mom,
we'll make sure you get home safe and

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:02.390
don't worry about the principal.

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:04.196
I'll just make sure you get
back to your family right now.

00:02:04.196 --> 00:02:09.810
And then Sade looks up at me and
she goes, he took my phone.

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:14.102
I said, who took your phone Sade?

00:02:14.102 --> 00:02:15.960
And she said, the man.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:20.125
I'm like okay, what man Sade?

00:02:20.125 --> 00:02:23.930
She said, the man with the gun.

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:28.280
Right before you came he put the gun
in my face and he took my phone.

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:32.080
At this point, I just have to stop.

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:38.900
I look around me, it's broad daylight,
it's lunchtime just like it is now.

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:44.560
I hear laughter in the distance,an
argument down the street,and here we are,

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:49.410
sitting on the steps of a church,
and a young girl morning her father

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:52.490
had just been robbed at gunpoint for
her cell phone.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:03:00.070
And, in that moment,
I realized there's no education reform,

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:02.500
there's no large scale theory of change or

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:07.070
impact model, there's no design thinking
white boards or post it notes, no case

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:11.406
study that would have ever prepared me for
how to react in that moment.

00:03:11.406 --> 00:03:16.660
So instead Sade leaned on my shoulder and

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:19.490
cried and I tried not to cry.

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:26.940
The next time I turned and looked at Sade,
I felt like I saw the world in her eyes.

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:36.350
The world she was living in seemed so
dark,so unfair,so scary but

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:41.500
the world I saw living in her was
when dominated by her godlike

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:46.650
personal strength and that was then that
I realize a greater truth about life.

00:03:48.428 --> 00:03:53.370
We all may live in this world but
we choose how we let the world live in us.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:04:00.970
If we fast forward it today,
Sade's mother is a drug addict and

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:05.690
to this day,
I have no idea where in the world Sade is.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:12.442
Anyone looking at her records would see
her poor grades, her poor behavior,

00:04:12.442 --> 00:04:16.689
her lack of attendance rate or
even her dropout rate at this point but

00:04:16.689 --> 00:04:21.702
what the records don't show is that I
have seen something divine in that child.

00:04:24.889 --> 00:04:29.645
Today, I'm not speaking to you as
the future business leaders, and

00:04:29.645 --> 00:04:34.899
entrepreneurs and policymakers,
which I know you all will become one day.

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:41.593
Instead I'm coming to you
humbly under the most

00:04:41.593 --> 00:04:46.697
basic common denominator that connects me,

00:04:46.697 --> 00:04:51.129
you, Mark Zuckerberg, Beyonce, and

00:04:51.129 --> 00:04:54.640
even Sade/g,our humanity.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:01.520
You see, at the GSB, we talk about
changing the world as if the world is

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:06.210
some vague, distant thing that we will
magically reenter after we graduate.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:11.070
But the truth about the world
is this: we are it,

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:16.230
the world is now, the world is here.

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:23.660
It is so easy to get caught up in school,
work, politics, and the very visible

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:27.210
differences that divide and separate
everyone sitting in this room today.

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:31.730
But if we want to take,
if we want to change the world

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:35.620
we have to be willing to look beyond
what's visible to the eye in other people.

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:40.160
Now, you may call this seeing God,
or the divine, in someone.

00:05:41.530 --> 00:05:43.860
You may call this acknowledging
someone's humanity.

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:49.270
Whatever we call it, we have to choose to
see something greater in ourselves and

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:53.630
other people because in
the absence of something greater

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:56.210
we miss out on the bigger
picture about life.

00:05:57.790 --> 00:06:00.000
That life is not about us.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:06.530
Life is about the value we create and
the legacy we'll leave behind and

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:10.270
if we don't see anything
greater in ourselves or

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:15.660
the people around us, our legacy
will end as soon as our lives end.

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:20.260
But if we take the time to see and

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:25.910
believe in something beyond ourselves and
align our work with a higher cause or

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:30.030
a higher purpose not only will
our lives be much richer,

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:35.760
but the work we do will live forever.

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:41.390
So how do we begin seeing more
in other people and ourselves?

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:45.990
Well, the answer is, we're going to have
to rediscover our sense of wonder and

00:06:45.990 --> 00:06:46.749
awe in the world.

00:06:48.250 --> 00:06:52.490
Albert Einstein once said he
who can no longer wonder and

00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:56.700
stand wrapped in awe is as good as dead,
his eyes are closed.

00:06:56.700 --> 00:07:02.340
As a child, it's very natural for us to
wonder and stand in awe of the world.

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:07.909
But as adults, we tend to assume that we
know everything that the world's about.

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:13.960
Wonder is defined as a heightened State of
Consciousness, brought upon by something

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:20.320
so beautiful, something so rare, so
unexpected, something that is a marvel.

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:23.240
It was a wonder for

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:27.670
me, to see the strength in Sade,
as she mourned the passing of her father.

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:32.360
Today, it is a wonder that I'm even
able to talk in front of you all.

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:35.090
And for some of us in the room,

00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:38.340
it was a marvel to even get
out of bed this morning.

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:44.080
Wonder has been shown to be one of
the most important emotional foundations

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:49.180
underlying optimum mental health and
not only is it linked to improved health,

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:53.240
but the capacity for experiencing wonder
and awe has been linked to improve

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:57.570
our abilities to build stronger,
more cohesive teams and communities.

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:03.270
So my challenge to you today, go home and

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:07.090
write down one wonder you
saw in someone else today.

00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:10.060
One wonder that enriched your
understanding of your world.

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:15.892
I promise if you do this, you'll
experience the world in a much deeper and

00:08:15.892 --> 00:08:18.213
richer way than you ever have before.

00:08:21.684 --> 00:08:24.660
I have realized that I may never
hear back from Sade again.

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:29.260
I may never have the answers to
the questions that I texted her.

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:34.970
But what I do know is that my life is so
much richer.

00:08:34.970 --> 00:08:37.650
Because I took the time
to see the world in her.

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:42.873
What will people say
when they look at you?

00:08:42.873 --> 00:08:43.777
Thank you.

00:08:43.777 --> 00:08:46.310
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:46.310 --> 00:08:51.039
[MUSIC]

